id: recipe.high-throughput-telemetry
kind: recipe
title: High-throughput telemetry collector
aliases: telemetry collector, metrics pipeline, tracing collector, high volume observability
question: how would you design a service that collects telemetry from a whole fleet?
tags: architecture, observability, scale
properties:
  - batches instead of per-sample writes
  - loss tolerated and explicitly bounded
  - cardinality controlled at the edge
  - ingestion decoupled from storage
invariant: Aggregate early, batch always, bound every buffer, and accept deliberate loss -- telemetry must never be able to block the applications it observes.
answer_30s: Aggregate in-process, batch and bound buffers at the agent, enforce cardinality limits at the collector, and let the pipeline shed load because telemetry is not a system of record.
flow: |
  app (aggregate counters/histograms in-process)
     |
  node agent (batch, bounded buffer, drop on overflow)
     |
  collector tier (cardinality limits, sampling, aggregation)
     |
  time-series / trace store (compaction, retention tiers)
     |
  dashboards and alerts
compose:
  - design.telemetry-pipeline => aggregation, batching and loss-tolerance as design rules
  - design.observability => the four signals and their cost models
  - design.backpressure => bounded buffers with an explicit drop policy
  - design.load-shedding => shed samples, never block the application
  - design.bounded-queue => buffer sizes chosen deliberately per hop
  - design.worker-pool => collector concurrency and batch sizes tuned to the store
  - k8s.daemon-set => one agent per node, including nodes added later
  - k8s.resources.requests => agents get reserved capacity so they cannot starve apps
  - k8s.hpa => scale the collector tier on ingest rate and queue depth
  - rust.bounded-concurrency => batch flushes with a cap
  - rust.tracing => structured fields with bounded cardinality
failure_modes:
  - unbounded label cardinality from user-controlled values (the usual root cause)
  - agents blocking the application to flush telemetry
  - no sampling, so the store's write path saturates during an incident (exactly when it is needed most)
  - per-sample network calls instead of batching
follow_ups:
  - how do you keep telemetry alive during an incident that also breaks the collector?
related:
  - design.telemetry-pipeline
  - design.observability
  - recipe.event-ingestion
sources:
  - https://opentelemetry.io/docs/collector/
  - https://prometheus.io/docs/practices/naming/
